350 World Psychiatry 25:3 - October 2026
SPECIAL ARTICLE
An etiological approach to psychosis: potential benefits
for treatment, research and prevention
Robin M. Murray1,2, Dominic Oliver1-3, Aikaterini Dima1,2,4,5, Luis Alameda1,2
1Department of Psychosis Studies, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology and Neuroscience, King’s College London, London, UK; 2NIHR Biomedical Research Centre, South London
and Maudsley NHS Foundation Trust, London, UK; 3Department of Psychiatry, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK; 4NIHR Oxford Health Biomedical Research Centre, Oxford,
UK; 5Oxleas NHS Foundation Trust, London, UK
Current treatment of psychosis focuses on antipsychotic medication, and rarely addresses the factors that have contributed to the onset and persistence of
the disorder. We suggest that the most useful way of facilitating treatment, research and prevention is to identify the causal risk factors to which psychotic
patients have been exposed. Operating on a background of genetic vulnerability, the main types of environmental risk factors for psychosis comprise
impaired neurodevelopment, substance abuse, and social adversity. In most patients with psychosis, striatal dopamine dysfunction, often mediated by
disrupted gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)ergic and glutamatergic signaling in the hippocampus, appears to be the final common pathway from
those distal risk factors to positive symptoms. There are clinical and biological differences among individuals who develop psychosis following exposure
to the different risk factors. For example, those with impaired neurodevelopment are more likely to show minor physical anomalies or soft neurological
signs, cognitive deficits, and brain structural abnormalities. Individualized interventions need to be offered to those patients who have been exposed to
particular risk factors. For example, in addition to antipsychotic medication, patients who continue to use cannabis should be offered cognitive behavioral/
motivational therapy to decrease their substance consumption, while patients who were exposed to child abuse could be offered trauma-based therapy,
and antidepressants as appropriate. Of course, many patients have been exposed to more than one type of risk factor. Such patients deserve therapeutic
action directed to each of those risk pathways. In addition to promoting individualized treatment, attention to etiology could benefit research, allowing
the study of psychological and biological characteristics of patients whose psychosis appears to have resulted predominantly from one particular risk
factor, and could open the door to universal and selective preventive strategies to decrease psychosis incidence through reducing exposure to risk factors.
Key words: Psychosis, genetic vulnerability, environmental risk factors, neurodevelopmental impairment, substance abuse, social adversity, stri
atal dopamine dysfunction, individualized interventions, preventive strategies
(World Psychiatry 2026;25:350–358)
Current treatment for psychosis focuses on blocking or modu
lating the dopamine D2 receptor1. As Maj et al2 have pointed out,
“the reality in many clinical contexts worldwide is that the patient
will just receive an antipsychotic medication plus some psycho
social support”. Antipsychotics do, of course, help most psychotic
patients, but meta analyses show that only about 20 40% achieve
long term recovery from a first episode3, and up to 30% come to be
regarded as treatment resistant4. Thus, an approach simply focus
ing on excess dopamine signaling is not sufficient.
When we compare psychiatry with general medicine, we see
that, while psychiatric disorders continue to be diagnosed on the
basis of signs and symptoms, many somatic diseases have been
delineated by cause. The futility of relying purely on symptomatol
ogy became apparent when conditions such as tuberculosis and
syphilis were shown to present a wide range of manifestations
which were unified by having a single identifiable cause.
Many of the diseases that affect society today are multifactorial
in cause. If we take coronary artery disease (CAD) as an example,
immediate treatment addresses the thrombosis in the coronary
artery, the final common pathway which mediates the cardiac
muscle damage. However, this does not mean that cardiologists
are disinterested in the causes of the thrombosis. On the contrary,
they know that hyperlipidaemia, hypertension, diabetes mellitus,
cigarette smoking, obesity, and lack of exercise all increase risk of
CAD, and research has traced the mechanistic pathways leading
from those distal causes to the thrombosis in the coronary artery.
Furthermore, in clinical practice, once they have resolved the im
mediate life threatening problems, cardiologists seek out the ex
ternal and internal risk factor(s) which have led to that endpoint in
the individual patient, and attempt to address these too5.
In psychiatry, attention is rarely paid to the different causal fac
tors which contribute to the onset and persistence of a disorder. In
this paper, we suggest that the best way to optimize research into
psychosis and improve its treatment is to attend to these causal risk
factors. Such an approach may help to elucidate the underlying
mechanistic pathways which link distal causes to the individual pa
tient’s symptoms, and thus contribute to personalized treatment.
Moreover, focusing on causal factors opens up an avenue towards
prevention.
A FINAL COMMON PATHWAY TO PSYCHOSIS?
Is there a final common pathway to psychosis equivalent to the
coronary artery thrombosis in CAD? The best candidate is striatal
dopamine dysfunction. In 2007, Di Forti et al6 published a paper
entitled “Risk factors for schizophrenia – all roads lead to dopa
mine”, in which they suggested that susceptibility genes, obstetric
complications, substance abuse, and trauma could all induce do
pamine dysfunction. Howes and Kapur7 developed this further and
argued that increased presynaptic striatal dopamine is the “final
common pathway” leading to aberrant salience and schizophrenia.
Since 2009, we have learned that the striatal dopamine abnor
malities are often mediated by disrupted gamma aminobutyric
acid (GABA)ergic and glutamatergic signaling in the hippocam
pus810 . This has led to the influential hypothesis that imbalance
between inhibitory and excitatory signaling causes an increased
activity in midbrain dopaminergic neurons that project to the
World Psychiatry 25:3 - October 2026 351
striatum10,11. The resultant increased striatal dopamine signaling
leads to discrepancies between expected and perceived stimu
li12,13. These prediction errors are hypothesized to lead to false
associations and perceptions, and in turn to delusions and hallu
cinations14. This is in line with the evidence that pharmacological
treatments for the positive symptoms of psychosis reduce activity
at the dopamine D2 receptor, and that high dopamine synthesis
capacity predicts good response of positive symptoms to D2 recep
tor blockers15,16.
Unfortunately, as yet the presence of striatal dopamine excess
synthesis cannot be ascertained in a clinical setting, since positron
emission tomography (PET) scans are expensive and only avail
able in a small number of centres. There is an urgent need for a
more practical means of estimating striatal dopamine. A possible
candidate for this is neuromelanin sensitive magnetic resonance
imaging (MRI)17, whose cost is similar to that of structural MRI.
While excess dopamine synthesis in the striatum appears to be
the final common pathway underlying positive symptoms in most
patients with psychosis, it does not explain negative symptoms or
cognitive impairment. Dysfunction of other neurotransmitter sys
tems has been involved in this respect, including glutamate, GAB
Aergic and cholinergic dysfunction. Evidence points particularly to
glutamate, since phencyclidine (PCP) and ketamine, antagonists
of the glutamatergic N methyl D aspartate receptor, can induce
negative and cognitive symptoms in healthy individuals1820 . Fur
thermore, patients deemed resistant to antipsychotics do not show
the expected striatal dopaminergic excess, and some evidence
suggests that they exhibit glutamatergic abnormality21,22.
As with the risk factors for CAD, we now have relatively good
knowledge of the causal risk factors, sometimes termed compo
nent causes, which drive onset of psychosis. However, as yet, we
have a limited understanding of the neurochemical pathways be
tween the distal causal factors and the patients’ symptoms. Under
standing the mechanistic pathways which link the former to the
latter would allow psychiatrists to better tailor effective treatments
to the individual patient.
GENETIC RISK
Similarly to CAD, the genetic vulnerability to psychosis is mostly
polygenic23, with a significant overlap of polygenic risk between
schizophrenia and bipolar disorder (approximately two thirds of
genes overlap)24. A recent study has shown that genetic liability
which is unique to schizophrenia and not shared with bipolar dis
order is associated with poor cognition, while genetic liability uni
que to bipolar disorder is associated with better cognition25. This
confirms our proposal two decades ago that – on a background
of shared genetic predisposition to psychosis – schizophrenia,
but not bipolar disorder, is linked to additional genes or early in
sults which impair neurodevelopment26. Owen et al27 cited the oc
currence of copy number variants in schizophrenia as the most
obvious example of this, as the implicated variants are associated
with a range of developmental disorders, from learning disability
through epilepsy to schizophrenia.
Genome wide association studies (GWAS) of schizophrenia
particularly implicate genes involved in neurodevelopment and
in synaptic function, and those with high expression in excitatory
glutamatergic neurons from cerebral cortex and hippocampus,
as well as cortical inhibitory GABA interneurons23. As previously
mentioned, GABAergic and glutamatergic systems have important
effects on dopaminergic function, thus indicating that genetic var
iants in these systems may alter the regulation of dopamine signal
ing in psychosis. Recently, Sportelli et al28 identified a set of genes
which show dopaminergic selectivity. A higher polygenic risk score
on this set of genes predicted greater dopamine synthesis in the
striatum and greater striatal activation during reward anticipation.
The most common copy number variant found in people with
schizophrenia is the 22q11.2 deletion, with up to 25% of carriers
developing psychosis. In contrast, individuals with 22q11.2 du
plication are protected from risk of schizophrenia. Interestingly,
non psychotic individuals who carry the deletion show an excess
of striatal dopamine, while those with the duplication have lower
striatal dopamine than normal subjects29.
ENVIRONMENTAL RISK FACTORS
In this issue of the journal, Aymerich et al30 provide an umbrella
review of meta analyses concerning environmental risk factors for
psychosis, classified by the extent of supportive evidence. For the
purposes of this paper, we have compressed the risk factors which
they consider to have convincing, highly suggestive, or suggestive
evidence into three overall types: a) those indicating an impaired
neurodevelopment (e.g., minor physical anomalies, low premorbid
IQ, birthweight under 2,000 g, being small for gestational age, soft
neurological signs, smoking during pregnancy, maternal infection
during pregnancy, winter season of birth); b) substance abuse (es
pecially cannabis use 5 to 7 days per week); and c) social adversity
(e.g., childhood adversities, being a first or second generation im
migrant, Black African or Black Caribbean ethnicity in England).
Impaired neurodevelopment
Three groups of prenatal and perinatal risk factors have been
significantly associated with later psychosis: a) complications of
pregnancy; b) abnormal foetal growth and development; and c)
complications of delivery3032 .
Early hazards to the developing brain can cause neurodevelop
mental impairment33. For example, pre term birth or asphyxia can
result in smaller brain volumes and lower cognitive abilities34,35. In
1971, Mednick et al36 suggested that perinatal hypoxia might dam
age the hippocampus, thus increasing risk of psychosis. In accord
with this, patients with schizophrenia who were exposed to obstet
ric difficulties have smaller hippocampi than both patients without
such exposure and normal subjects37,38.
FroudistWalsh et al 39 followed up babies who had been born
pre term into adulthood. PET scans showed that neonatal hippo
campal injury was associated with altered striatal dopamine syn20515545, 2026, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wps.70079 by Readcube (Labtiva Inc.), Wiley Online Library on [24/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
352 World Psychiatry 25:3 - October 2026
thesis, thus providing a potential mechanism linking early brain
injury to adult psychotic illness. Animal studies have shown that
perinatal administration of methylazoxymethanol acetate (MAM)
in pregnant rats, or lesioning the hippocampus in neonatal rats,
both lead to behavior considered psychosis like as well as to dopa
minergic abnormalities in adult rats40,41.
Prenatal maternal infection has also been associated with later
psychosis42. Two models of psychosis rely on infection during ges
tation in rodents: maternal gestational exposure to the human in
fluenza virus43, and administration of the viral mimic polyinosinic
polycytidylic acid44. Both models result in a reduced number of
GABAergic hippocampal neurons. The consequent inhibition
results in both glutamatergic and dopaminergic dysfunction down
stream4547 .
Finally, recent evidence suggests that excessive pruning of glu
tamatergic synapses during adolescence is not only associated
with decreased grey matter volumes, but may also alter the balance
between cortical excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmission in
schizophrenia48.
Substance abuse
There is good evidence that psychostimulants, such as amphet
amines and cocaine, can induce psychosis. Indeed, methamphet
amine induced psychosis is a major problem in some Pacific coun
tries, Australia, South Africa, and parts of North America49,50. These
substances are all dopamine receptor agonists, directly exacerbat
ing the dopaminergic abnormalities seen in psychosis, as well as
triggering psychosis onset.
Epidemiological studies have consistently shown a relation
ship between cannabis use and the development of psychosis,
with increased risks ranging from 1.5 to 951,52. Initially, objections
were raised to the hypothesis that cannabis use was causal to psy
chosis, with sceptics citing possible abuse of other substances, or
self medication for psychotic or prodromal symptoms. However,
all of these hypotheses, as well as the idea that cannabis use was
a consequence of genetic predisposition to schizophrenia, have
been disproven53. Furthermore, meta analysis has demonstrated
a clear dose response relationship between the level of use, as well
as the potency of cannabis preparation, and the risk for psycho
sis54. Importantly, continued use by individuals with psychosis is
associated with increased risk of recurrence of psychotic symp
toms, and rehospitalizations55.
Animal studies show that the endogenous cannabinoid system
interacts with the dopamine system in the basal ganglia. Tetrahy
drocannabinol (THC) stimulates CB1 receptors, which modulate
pre synaptic glutamate and GABA release. In this way, THC alters
the balance of excitation and inhibition reaching dopamine cells,
with an increase in firing and resultant elevations of dopamine re
lease56.
As both the endocannabinoid and dopamine systems are ac
tively developing during adolescence, it is likely that any interfer
ence with this signaling during that period will result in psycho
pathological phenotypes later in life. Thus, in juvenile rodents,
THC disinhibits prefrontal cortex network function, with conse
quent hyperactivity of subcortical dopaminergic activity leading to
a range of cognitive and affective phenotypes that resemble those
observed in schizophrenia57.
THC administration to humans is associated with medial tem
poral and striatal activation which is specifically correlated with
transient psychotic symptoms58,59. Some60, although not all61,
studies in healthy volunteers have reported that acute administra
tion of THC leads to increased striatal dopamine release. More re
cently, Ahrens et al62 have used neuromelanin sensitive MRI, which
– as previously noted – reflects dopamine synthesis and release, to
study cannabis users. The users showed higher neuromelanin sig
nal in the substantia nigra/ventral tegmental area, as did people
with early schizophrenia.
Social adversity
In a seminal meta analysis, Varese et al63 reported that childhood
adversity (abuse, neglect or bullying) was associated with increased
risk for adult psychosis (overall odds ratio, OR=2.78). Dragioti et al64
further estimated that 38% of schizophrenia cases could be pre
vented if childhood trauma were eradicated. These findings have
been confirmed by the above mentioned umbrella review by Aym
erich et al30, which reports that childhood adversities are associated
with an increased risk for psychosis (OR=2.87), and are the poten
tially modifiable risk factor with the highest population attributable
fraction (40.69%). Psychological mediators proposed to explain the
association between childhood adversity and psychotic disorders
include negative cognitive schemas about the self and others, as
well as symptoms of post traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)65,66.
A meta analysis by Beards et al67 showed 3 fold increased odds
that adults with psychosis had experienced adverse life events in
the period prior to onset. Psychotic patients are also more likely
to live alone, to be unemployed, and to live in overcrowded con
ditions, not only at first contact with psychiatric services but up to
five years prior to the onset of psychosis68.
The umbrella review by Aymerich et al30 reports an increased
incidence of psychosis among first and second generation im
migrants (risk ratio, RR = 2.069 and 1.845, respectively); people of
Black African (RR=4.891) and Black Caribbean (RR=5.224) ethnic
ity in England; and North African immigrants in Europe (incidence
rate ratio, IRR=2.22). Psychosocial stress, partly generated through
discrimination6971 , may be a key driver of psychosis risk in such
individuals7274 .
Chronic activation of the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal (HPA)
axis, with high basal/unstimulated cortisol levels, is reported fol
lowing repeated exposure to social stressors, including in individ
uals with psychosis7578 . Conversely, cortisol responses to awak
ening and to social stressor tasks are attenuated among individ
uals with psychosis relative to controls7981 , since the high basal cor
tisol levels reduce the ability of the HPA axis to mount an appropri
ate stress response8285 .
Psychotic patients who had been subject to severe abuse in
childhood or who have been immigrants show elevated dopa20515545, 2026, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wps.70079 by Readcube (Labtiva Inc.), Wiley Online Library on [24/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
