mine synthesis in the associative striatum in adulthood86,87. Selten
et al88 have suggested that the long term experience of adversity
and exclusion (causing social defeat) impacts the dopamine sys
tem. In accord with this, Morgan et al89 hypothesized a socio
developmental pathway in which exposure to adversity and trau
ma impacts on the stress response and dopamine system to create
an enduring liability to psychosis.
CLINICAL ADVANTAGES OF AN APPROACH TO
PSYCHOSIS BASED ON ETIOLOGY
The above evidence suggests that individuals vulnerable to psy
chosis inherit genes which predispose them to develop an imbal
ance between excitatory (glutamatergic) and inhibitory (GABAer
gic) signaling. This results in increased activity of midbrain dopa
minergic neurons that project to the striatum.
The abnormal dopamine signaling can then be further exacer
bated by three main types of environmental risk factors: neurode
velopmental impairment, frequent substance (in particular canna
bis) use, and social adversity. In addition, deviant neurodevelop
ment involving abnormal glutamate connectivity may contribute
to negative symptoms, cognitive deficits, and non response to
treatment with dopamine blockers.
Bearing such an etiological model in mind could enable psy
chiatrists to achieve a more personalized approach to treatment of
psychosis2. Obviously, all people with psychosis should be offered
antipsychotics. But, in addition, it is important that the underlying
risk factors which are driving the psychosis be also addressed, if
possible. While we acknowledge that an individual with psychosis
may have been exposed to several risk factors, and that there are
cases in which no risk factor can be identified, we shall concentrate
on the characteristics and treatment of those prototypical patients
in whom exposure to one particular risk factor appears to have
been crucial to the development of psychosis.
Prototypical patients with impaired neurodevelopment
Psychotic patients who have been subject to early neurode
velopmental insults tend to show characteristic epiphenomena.
These include minor physical anomalies and soft neurological
signs, both indicative of altered foetal development90,91. Many ex
hibit low premorbid IQ92. This is found in patients with copy num
ber variants, but is more frequently polygenic93, and may also be
consequent upon obstetric complications94. These patients pres
ent with early age at illness onset95, and more neurocognitive96
and MRI abnormalities33,97,98. Patients who have been exposed to
early environmental hazards are particularly likely to present neg
ative symptoms and to have a poor outcome99,100.
The Lewis Murray Obstetric Events Scale101 is a 15 item tool for
assessing prenatal and perinatal events, especially in individu
als with psychosis. It includes three sections (Lewis categories A,
B and C). Category A covers risk factors occurring during preg
nancy, such as maternal infections, Rh incompatibility, severe
pre eclampsia, bleeding before delivery, threatened miscarriage.
Category B covers risk factors concerning foetal growth and de
velopment, such as twin delivery, abnormal gestational age (pre
term, post term), low birthweight (<2,500 g), physical anomalies.
Category C covers difficulties in delivery, such as premature mem
brane rupture, very long/short labor, umbilical cord prolapse,
complicated C section, abnormal foetal presentation, forceps use,
prolonged incubator stay. Each event can be rated as “definite”,
“equivocal” or “absent”. The assessment is usually based on mater
nal interview, but it is better if the patient’s obstetric records can
be rated. The use of this tool can be suggested in clinical practice
to identify individuals with psychosis who are likely to have suffer
ed from neurodevelopmental impairment.
These patients should also have an MRI scan and a neuropsy
chological assessment. Cognitive deficits are often associated with
poor response to antipsychotics and designation as treatment re
sistant102, as are brain structural abnormalities103,104. Treatment
resistant patients do not show the characteristic striatal dopami
nergic excess on both PET scans and neuromelanin sensitive MRI.
Rather, they may show increased glutamate in the anterior cin
gulate cortex21,22. It is, therefore, not surprising that they do not re
spond to conventional dopamine blockers.
Psychotic patients with neurodevelopmental damage should
receive cognitive remediation105,106 to maximize their intellectual
function, and assertive community support to help them negotiate
the complexities of society such as how to obtain social benefits
or manage their money107. Support to family carers is invaluable.
Clozapine should be considered early in the course of the psycho
sis – for example, by early intervention services108,109.
Prototypical patients with frequent substance use
Patients who develop psychosis following frequent substance
use show a different profile from those who suffered neurodevel
opmental insult. They tend to have had good premorbid social
and academic function, and have an average or high premorbid
IQ110,111. In addition, they present to clinical services earlier than
non using psychotic patients112, and at onset their MRI scans usu
ally do not show evidence of neurodevelopmental damage113.
Their outcome depends on whether they continue or stop their
drug use55,114.
For screening of cannabis use in the last six months, the Canna
bis Use Disorders Identification Test Revised (CUDIT R)115 can
be suggested for use in clinical practice. This tool is designed for
self administration and includes eight items. The Cannabis Expe
riences Questionnaire (CEQ)116, whose original version includes
56 items (while short forms include 42 or 14 items), can be used
for a more in depth assessment of experiences related to canna
bis assumption. The ASSIST (Alcohol, Smoking and Substance
Involvement Screening Test)117, including eight questions con
cerning use of various substances, can be suggested for a broader
screening of drug use. Furthermore, specific urine or blood tests
can be used to identify the assumption of various substances.
The choice of the antipsychotic has a bearing on the likelihood20515545, 2026, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wps.70079 by Readcube (Labtiva Inc.), Wiley Online Library on [24/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
