of patients with substance abuse to relapse into drug taking. Sub
stance dependent patients show low dopamine in the ventral stri
atum, which exacerbates drive to use substances. Craving, for ex
ample for cannabis, can be exacerbated by antipsychotics which
cause high D2 receptor blockade118. Partial D2 receptor agonists,
such as aripiprazole and cariprazine, are associated with a better
outcome118. Pharmacological help with a preparation such as Sa
tivex (containing low dose THC and cannabidiol in a spray) to cov
er substance withdrawal can also be helpful, particularly given the
potential of cannabis withdrawal to precipitate or exacerbate psy
chosis symptoms119.
Patients with psychosis associated with substance use frequent
ly fall between psychiatric and drug addiction services. Therefore,
Di Forti et al120 established a specialized clinic for psychotic pa
tients who use cannabis, which offers one to one therapy with
motivational interviewing and support as well as attendance at a
weekly peer group. A reduction in dependence was observed, with
74% of participants achieving abstinence and 70% returning to
work or education.
Prototypical patients with a history of social adversity
Child abuse is associated with more severe positive and depres
sive symptoms; neglect is related to more severe negative symp
toms121; and both abuse and neglect are associated with poor so
cial functioning122.
The Trauma and Life Events (TALE) checklist123 is a 20 item
scale covering physical/sexual abuse, emotional neglect, bullying,
feeling unsafe in childhood, unusual experiences (voices, visions),
and distress from mental health service contact (restraint, assault).
Its use can be suggested in clinical practice to identify those psy
chotic patients who have a history of trauma.
Treatment focused on psychotic patients who have suffered
childhood adversity is at an early stage. Trials using trauma fo
cused cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and eye movement de
sensitization and reprocessing (EMDR) have been conducted
124,125. These trials are based on the hypothesis that treating PTSD
symptoms may not only improve these, but also the related psy
chosis. Indeed, dissociation puts reality testing under pressure and
may alter the ability of an individual to distinguish post traumatic
intrusions as such, externalizing them in the form of a perceptual
abnormality. This raises the intriguing possibility that addressing
the mechanism may improve the outcome as well. Preliminary
results from these trials are very encouraging126.
Much research in recent years has focused on an affective path
way to psychosis. This model postulates that people exposed to
social adversity will often develop low mood and anxiety, which
in turn makes them vulnerable to developing delusions and hal
lucinations127. People with psychosis following social adversity
can have significant levels of depression and anxiety, that may be
enough to affect reality testing and precipitate onset or relapse
128,129. Therefore, among people with trauma and psychosis, low
mood and anxiety – even at sub diagnostic level – should be care
fully assessed, and treated not only with CBT but also antidepres
sants where appropriate, along with antipsychotics.
Exposure to several risk factors
Clinicians will recognize the prototypical risk patients that we
have described above, but will also point out that many patients
with psychosis have been exposed to several risk factors. These
may be of similar type (e.g., those born prematurely may have also
suffered asphyxia and other perinatal complications; or those who
were abused as a child may have then suffered adolescent bullying
as well as adverse life events in adult life). But individuals may have
also been exposed to different types of risk factors. For example,
those exposed to childhood adversity at home may gravitate in
their adolescence to street gangs and exposure to substance use130.
They will then share some of the characteristics of those from the
childhood adversity group and those from the substance abuse
group.
Patients who have been exposed to several risk factors deserve
therapeutic attention directed to each of these factors in the same
way that cardiologists will address several risk factors for CAD –
such as hyperlipidaemia, cigarette smoking, and lack of exercise
– in the same individual. This situation requires a flexible and
integrative approach where the treatment priorities can be set
hierarchically based on the predominant feature (e.g., focus on
substance abuse could take place first, and addressing the con
sequences of social adversity could occur once substance abuse
ceases or at least decreases significantly).
Of course, when considering the effects of several environmen
tal risk factors, it is important to recall that many patients with psy
chosis have inherited genetic vulnerability to illness. Studies have
been examining the relationship between genetic and environ
mental risk. For example, genetic risk for schizophrenia and heavy
cannabis use appear to act as independent risk factors for psycho
sis. Many people with a high polygenic risk score for schizophrenia
develop psychosis without ever using cannabis, while others with
a low polygenic risk score only develop psychosis following very
heavy cannabis use. However, in the majority of patients, the two
factors combine to push the individual over the threshold for ex
pressing psychosis53.
Epigenetic changes, especially DNA methylation, may help clar
ify how exposure to more than one risk factor contributes to the
development of psychosis131. For example, individuals exposed
to childhood adversity are both more likely to subsequently abuse
cannabis and then to develop psychosis. An ongoing study is ex
amining whether this susceptibility to the psychotogenic effects of
cannabis is mediated by the changes in methylation consequent
upon childhood adversity132.
ADVANTAGES FOR RESEARCH
In addition to allowing individualization of treatment, the a
doption of an etiological approach should also facilitate research,
since the usual strategy of studying all patients diagnosed as hav20515545, 2026, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wps.70079 by Readcube (Labtiva Inc.), Wiley Online Library on [24/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
World Psychiatry 25:3 - October 2026 355
ing schizophrenia according to the DSM 5 or ICD 11 criteria/re
quirements leads to sample heterogeneity, with the inclusion of
patients whose psychoses have resulted from quite different risk
factors. It is likely that, for example, neuropsychological or imaging
characteristics of patients with neurodevelopmental impairment
are obscured by being merged with patients with whom they have
little in common etiologically. A more productive research strat
egy would be to identify prototypical patients whose psychosis ap
pears to have resulted predominantly from one particular risk
factor and then study the psychological and biological character
istics of these patients. This would also facilitate research into the
underlying mechanisms, linking a particular risk factor to the dis
order and allowing to explore how it alters clinical trajectories and
outcome.
This strategy could also result in more productive clinical trials.
We have already seen how CBT for psychotic patients with PTSD
symptoms is reported to be very effective124, while its effective
ness for psychosis as a whole remains disputed. Another possible
approach stems from the evidence that glutamatergic abnormali
ties may be particularly important in patients with neurodevel
opmental impairment, who, as we have seen, are more likely to
be diagnosed as treatment resistant. Glutamatergic drugs have
been repeatedly tried and failed in trials of psychotic patients as a
whole. It may be more productive to examine their effectiveness in
patients with evidence of neurodevelopmental impairment, with
treatment resistant schizophrenia, or in those shown to have glu
tamatergic abnormalities on magnetic resonance spectroscopy.
Such approaches can be supported through the use of platform
trials133, which are designed to test several different putative treat
ments using a common control condition. Platform trials allow
participants to be stratified according to exposure to different risk
factors, enabling the investigation of differential treatment effects a
cross predefined subgroups. As evidence accumulates, arms can
be dropped (no effect seen) or added (promising molecule iden
tified). Thus, platform trials offer an efficient means of testing
targeted treatments in psychotic patients exposed to different risk
factors, addressing heterogeneity that may obscure effects in con
ventional trials.
It is worth reflecting on the fact that the importance of indi
vidual risk factors for psychosis may vary both geographically and
temporally. The varying prevalence of different risk factors – such
as substance abuse – in different geographical and temporal con
texts suggests that the clinical and biological profile of psychosis
may not be constant across all countries and all times; rather it
may reflect the frequency of the different risk factors in the specific
situation. Thus, researchers should not necessarily expect psycho
sis in low income countries to have the same characteristics as in
highincome settings.
OPENING THE DOOR TOWARDS PREVENTION
Preventive approaches are common in medicine and often in
volve putting in place strategies to limit exposure to a particular risk
factor. Universal prevention (i.e., interventions that target the gen
eral population) has proved valuable: an example is the successful
public health campaign to reduce tobacco smoking in many West
ern societies.
A focus on causal risk factors for psychosis could inform such
programmes. Thus, for neurodevelopmental abnormalities, this
could involve improving pre and perinatal care or increasing
access to family planning services to reduce risk of pregnancy and
delivery complications.
In many countries, increases in the frequency and potency of
cannabis use have been followed by reports of an increase in the
incidence of psychosis134. Public health policies could be used to
reduce such harms. These are challenging to implement, given the
influence of the illegal cannabis market and the rapid expansion
of commercial delivery of cannabis, but are much needed. Some
school based interventions have shown that it is possible to im
prove young people’s attitudes to substances and mental health.
Iceland is one of the few countries to have successfully implement
ed this strategy nationally135.
To our knowledge, no universal preventive trials have been con
ducted to demonstrate the impact of diminishing exposure to risk
factors specifically to reduce the incidence of psychosis136. How
ever, a randomized controlled trial among 13,035 adolescents was
carried out in Bihar, India, testing whether a health promotion
intervention could improve the emotional climate in 75 schools.
Eight months later, children who received the intervention reported
substantially better emotional climate in their schools than control
children, as well as improvements in depression, bullying and vio
lence137. Building on the success of that trial, mental health workers
in Tokyo have persuaded the city government to test a similar inter
vention across four boroughs138.
More selective strategies (i.e., interventions that target groups
that are at risk due to specified risk factors) are often implemented
within medicine. An obvious method to address neurodevelop
mental risk would involve ensuring that mothers with psychosis
have optimal antenatal care and close supervision at the time of
birth, so that the genetically susceptible baby is not exposed to pro
longed labor or hypoxia. Similarly, reducing substance use in peo
ple already using substances through effective addiction treatment
could decrease risk of developing psychosis. Finally, in identified
cases of child abuse, family interventions can be used to improve
parenting practices139.
CONCLUSION
A narrow focus on antipsychotic medication is insufficient to
address the complexity of psychosis, which arises from the in
teraction between genetic vulnerability and environmental risk
factors. By identifying and targeting the specific causal exposures
that contribute to the onset and maintenance of psychosis, clini
cians and researchers can better understand the heterogeneity
in the onset of the disorder, its presentation and its outcomes.
Recognizing that these distinct risk factors are likely to con
verge on common dopaminergic mechanisms, yet are associated
with different clinical and biological profiles, supports the need20515545, 2026, 3, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wps.70079 by Readcube (Labtiva Inc.), Wiley Online Library on [24/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
356 World Psychiatry 25:3 - October 2026
for more personalized and mechanism informed interventions.
Integrating pharmacological treatment with psychological and
social interventions tailored to an individual’s risk history not
only holds promise for improving clinical outcomes, but also pro
vides a framework for developing preventive strategies aimed at
reducing exposure to modifiable risk factors.
